ABSTRACT
The increased global demand for animal-based products results in generation of larger volume of protein-rich waste (PRW). Developing novel methods for efficient and eco-friendly conversion of these wastes is of great importance. The present study suggests a novel approach using black soldier fly larvae (BSFL) to recycle PRW treated with dietary acetic acid, which potentially reduces the NH3, N2O, and CH4 emissions. Compared to the control, applying 2 % dietary acetic acid significantly increased the larval biomass yield and areal biomass productivity by 24.4 % and 26.3 %, respectively. The study exhibited superior biodiesel characteristics with improved cetane numbers (60.08–61.17). The suggested route estimated reduction of the global warming potential (GWP) to 2.67 g CO2-eq per kg dry larval biomass. There was a synergistic effect of dietary acetic acid to achieve a well-balanced diet for BSFL growth and NH3 recycling while reducing greenhouse gases (GHGs) emissions, thereby enhancing the achievement of cleaner biodiesel production from PRW. Overall, this study provides insight into BSFL as a potential organism for efficient recycling of PRW from industrial animal byproducts for enhanced biodiesel recovery and reduced GHGs emissions, which could support the low-carbon economy and sustainability objectives.
Introduction
Demand for agrarian products (crops and livestock) is increasing rapidly due to the growing global population and the high cost of agricultural inputs. The inability of the majority of developing nations to meet their food requirements poses a threat to the security of energy, food, and feed; with raised environmental concerns of waste generation [1]. With a projected 10-billion-person population increase by 2050, food security will be jeopardized unless global food production is increased by at least 60 % above 2006 levels [2]. In this context, there has been 40 % increase in the animal meat consumption during the past ten years due to the growing global population [3]. The global meat production was estimated by 253 million tons in 2020; this included 57.7 million tons of beef, 95.8 million tons of pork, and 99.4 million tons of poultry [3]. This intensive dairy and meat products result in a lot of
byproducts and the death of many animals due to several factors like spread of diseases and infections, which lead to huge amount of meat is wasted between the farm and the plate. According to a report by Luckmann [4], more than 200 million animals die every year in France without being used as meat and 50 million male chicks are lost each year directly after they hatch, while about 200,000 male dairy calves die in Germany because they would produce too little meat. In 2018, China lost between 150 and 200 million pigs because of the African swine fever, a fatal infectious and lethal disease [5]. The outcome of this situation leads to a substantial rise in the accumulation of protein-rich waste (PRW). The wasted PRW from dead animals or animal byproducts is a highrisk material because it is a major source of disease transmission, release of greenhouse gases (GHGs) and ammonia (NH3), as well as waste resources [6]. Consequently, it is imperative to establish effective and
