sustainable management practices to mitigate the adverse impacts associated with the huge PRW. Among the newly techniques for disposing of PRW, the rendering is attractive technology applied for nonedible slaughterhouses by products such as bones, internal organs, and other meat wastes that are subjected to high-pressure and hightemperature during rendering process, typically 180 ◦C and 5 bars for 30 min to produce meat and bone meal (MBM) [6]. However, by feeding cows recycled MBM, mad cow disease or bovine spongiform encephalopathy can be transmitted [7]. In consequence, MBM is no longer permitted as a ruminant feed in the majority of the world’s nations. Due to its high nutrient content, MBM can also be utilized as a crop fertilizer or soil conditioner to promote plant growth [8]. However, this option cannot avoid losing a substantial quantity of valuable C and N. The lost nutrients will be dissolved into the water system or released into the atmosphere (as GHGs or ammonia). Application of this technique resulted in the loss of more than 70–74 % of the total original nitrogen, primarily as NH3 (9.6–46 % of the total nitrogen lost) emissions [9]. The loss of nutrients, transmission of pathogens, and/or resultant pollution require alternative sustainable and safe routes for using PRW/MBM, which should meet hygiene standards. The use of saprophagous insects for waste management coupled with biodiesel and/or protein feed production has attracted increasing attention in recent years [10,11]. Insect biomass has the potential to become a key component of a sustainable, circular agricultural production system by closing carbon and nutrient cycles, promoting food supply, and mitigating the climate crisis and biodiversity loss [12]. In addition, insect-based conversion of agri-biowaste, including waste protein, is an excellent recycling route and a holistic solution due to its fewer negative environmental impacts including less emissions of NH3 and GHGs, with relatively small carbon footprint in providing sustainable protein feed, lipids, and other nutrient supplements [13]. This approach meets the circular economy principles that paves the way for achieving many Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) [14]. Among these insects, black soldier fly larvae (Hermetia illucens L., Stratiomyidae, Diptera) (BSFL) have been employed in an efficient recycling biotechnology by being fed various decaying organic materials [15,16]. In comparison to other insects, such as yellow mealworms or houseflies, BSFL can efficiently convert organic waste materials due to the high appetite and population density, as well as the potential to simultaneously suppress the bacteria in the substrate to minimize odor production [17]. As BSFL contains 30 %–46 % protein [18] and 20 %43 % fat [19], it has been considered an alternative source for numerous bioproducts. Despite BSFL has been considered a viable method for managing and even recycling wide range of organic waste, the high nitrogen content in PRW results in significant ammonia emissions, accounting for ~9–40 % of the total nitrogen losses during BSFL biotransformation [20], which further affects the conversion process and has negative environmental impacts. Recent studies showed that GHGs and NH3 emissions during BSFL biotransformation can be controlled by several factors, including moisture content, feeding mode, pH, and temperature [21,22]. Addition of organic acids, including acetic acid, is among the most effective strategies to control and reduce the emissions of nitrogencontaining gases due to its inherent properties and prevalence in nature as an attractive additive to be used as urease inhibitor, it has been utilized to develop a natural, non-toxic method for mitigating NH3 and CH4 emissions, as well as odor from livestock waste [23]. In addition, it was recently discovered that acetic acid-containing dietary supplements improve metabolic homeostasis and larval growth in fruit flies fed on an extremely nutrient-deficient diet [24]. A recent study by Pang et al. [22] applied short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) (acetic acid and butyric acid) to BSFL diet consisting of pig manure with a corncob and confirmed that SCFAs could improve the larval biomass and their lipids content. In addition, Popa and Green [25] examined the impact of SCFAs on BSFL growth in a fluid phase (leachate), and reported that larvae could regulate the pH to a level within 2.5–9.0.
In terms of biofuels, biodiesel is discussed as a promising substitute for petroleum-based diesel because it can be made from a variety of lipid-rich biomass (waste), including terrestrial oil-crops, microalgae, animal fats, and waste cooking oil [26,27]. Alternative feedstocks have also been considered as potential sources of biodiesel, including BSFL [28]. It has the special advantages of being non-hazardous producing fewer undesirable emissions, being biodegradable, and having the potential to achieve global circular economy and carbon neutrality [29]. The main reason why biodiesel is the greatest substitute for petroleumbased diesel is because its fundamental qualities are comparable to those of diesel fuel. The creation of biodiesel from different types of biowaste by BSFL, however, has been touted as a viable way to resolve the food vs. fuel debate. Due to higher productivity of BSFL biomass, it is a promising platform organism for biowaste recycling into biodiesel production in line with microalgae and oleaginous yeast. Due to the fact that BSFL lipid production does not interfere with the food supply chain, they exhibit substantial potential as a promising and sustainable feedstock source for biodiesel production [30]. Considering the facts mentioned above, the inclusion of dietary acetic acid in the process of bioconversion by BSFL when using PRW can effectively capture ammonium, which reduces NH3 and GHGs emissions. Additionally, this practice could enhance the overall biological performance of BSFL and improve the efficiency of waste conversion. Consequently, it opens up opportunities for the establishment of novel circular supply chains that facilitate the recovery and recycling of nitrogen. So far, there is no existing research or investigation that specifically examined the impact of incorporating SCFAs, i.e. dietary acetic acid, during the process of PRW biotransformation by BSFL, and its impact on biodiesel recovery, GHGs/NH3 emissions. Accordingly, this study aimed to investigate the supplementation of various doses of acetic acid (0–4.0 %, w/w) into BSFL-based bioconversion to effectively recycle PRW for biodiesel production. The effect of dietary acetic acid on larval development and nutrient accumulation was estimated. In addition, the impact of dietary acetic acid on BSFL frass quality was examined by scanning electron microscope (SEM), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), and fluorescence spectroscopy analysis. The emission of NH3 and GHGs during BSFL growth was evaluated at optimal doses of acetic acid. Moreover, the BSFL biodiesel characteristics were evaluated at different acetic acid doses comparing to other feedstocks. This study aims to establish a dual-purpose solution by utilizing eco-friendly insect biotransformation to recycle PRW for biodiesel production, while simultaneously mitigating the emissions of GHGs and NH3.
Materials and methods
BSFL and raw materials
The PRW was provided as bovine powder of MBM by Qingdao national trade Co. LTD, China. Coarse wheat bran (WB) was obtained from a farm-owned wheat processing plant in Chengdu City, China. The characteristics of MBM and WB were measured and are presented in Table S1 (Supplementary data). The BSF eggs were obtained from Yunnan insect farm Co LTD, China, and hatched as described previously [28]. The BSFL rearing experiment was conducted at the New Energy and Environmental Laboratory (NEEL), Chengdu University, China as described in the following section.
Experimental design
About 120 g dry matter of the experimental substrate was prepared by mixing 50 g of MBM and 70 g of WB to reach a C/N ratio of 15 % to achieve a balanced diet as reported previously [31]. Batch BSFL rearing was conducted in a cylindrical glass reactor 12 × 18 cm (d/h) by introducing 300 BSFL (7-days-old, average 5.1 mg/larva) into the feed mixture at 70 % humidity and 25 ◦C [32]. In accordance with the initial investigations, different concentrations of dietary acetic acid [0.0
