Analytical procedures
Proximate and chemical analyses of MBM and WB including volatile solids (VS), total solids (TS), and ash contents were conducted following the ASTM E870-82 standard methods. The pH was measured after mixing 3 g of the substrate in 15 mL deionized water, followed by keeping it at room temperature for 1 h. Elements including C and N were measured on a dry weight basis using an element analyzer (Elementar Unicube, Germany). Total carbohydrates and proteins in the BSFL biomass were estimated using phenol–sulfuric acid method [34] and the Bradford method [35], respectively. Total lipids were extracted using Soxhlet apparatus by immersing 2 g of BSFL dry biomass in 250 mL of petroleum ether for 12 h, then the solvent was evaporated at 60 ◦C and the total lipid content was calculated gravimetrically per dry weight as described previously [28]. FTIR within the spectra range 4000–500
cm 1 (Nicolet Avatar 330, Thermo Electron Corporation, USA) was used to investigate the changes in the functional groups of molecules, while SEM (JSM 6700F, JEOL, UK) was applied to examine the micromorphological modifications of the substrates at the chosen ratios. After separating the larvae from the rearing medium, the frass samples were taken for drying at 70 ◦C till constant weight. The dried frass samples were taped up on dual-sided tape, placed on aluminium stubs, and a thin layer of gold (15 nm for 10 min) was sputtered on the mounted sample to limit electron modifying effects and increase sample conductivity to enhance SEM images quality. Samples that had been extracted for dissolved organic matter (DOM) were mixed with ultrapure water in a ratio of 1:10 (w/v, dry weight), shaken for 24 h at 25 ◦C, and then centrifuged for 20 min at 10,000 rpm. After centrifugation, the filtrates’ organic carbon content was measured using an automated TOC analyzer (Shimadzu TOC-V), then ultrapure water was used to dilute to
10 mg L–1. Using a fluorescence spectrophotometer (F-7000, Hitachi, Japan), the diluted solutions were used to analyze the excitationemission matrix (EEM) fluorescence spectroscopy.
