Fatty acid analysis and biodiesel characteristics
By employing KOH/methanol, the fatty acids in the lipid extracts were transesterified [36]. Fatty acid profile was analyzed by GC/MS at the conditions described in the previous study [33]. The key biodiesel characteristics, including unsaturation degree, specific gravity, cloud point, kinematic viscosity, cetane number, iodine value, and higher heating value were determined based on the fatty acid profile [37,38].
Statistical analysis
SPSS software was utilized to perform statistical analysis of the experimental data. One-way ANOVA was used to examine the effects of acetic acid on the physicochemical properties of the substrate and gas emissions. The significance level for all statistical analyses was set at 0.05.
Results and discussion
Growth performance
Nutritional balance in the culture medium was reported to have a major impact on BSFL development and bioconversion rate [32]. The results of the current study showed that the growth curves of BSFL are significantly affected by the addition of acetic acid at various doses (Fig. S1, Supplementary data). Larvae developed rapidly by increasing the acetic acid dose initially, reaching the highest weight of 158.5 mg
larva–1 on the 9th day using 2 % acetic acid, which was 17.3 % higher than the control. However, further increase in acetic acid dosage caused reduction of the fresh weight, reaching the lowest recorded value of
126.7 mg larva–1 at 4 % acetic acid. Therefore, 2 % acetic acid showed the highest recorded biomass yield gaining of 45.9 g and 14.7 g fresh and dry weight, respectively (Fig. 1A). Application of acetic acid to the substrate greatly impacted the survival rate of BSFL as well. Larval survival rate (LSR) is used as a key parameter for substrate feeding quality, where the highest LSR of 97 % was recorded at 2 % acetic acid, which was 3.2 % higher than the control (Fig. 1A). It has been found that BSFL survival rate and final larval productivity are significantly influenced by the feeding conditions and chemical composition of the applied diet. Gao et al. [39] reported that the survival rate of BSFL reared on wheat brane or fermented maize straw was 93 %. Barragan-Fonseca et al. [41] recorded BSFL survival rates ranged from 87.1 % to 98.1 % when it was fed on three levels of dietary nutrients (chicken feed). The relatively high LSR recorded in the present study confirms the positive impact of acetic acid added to PRW for enhanced larval growth. As a result of the enhanced larval growth at 2 % acetic acid, it demonstrated the highest larval biomass gain rate and areal larval
biomass productivity of 5.61 mg larva–1 d–1 and 162.3 g m 2 d–1, which were 24.40 % and 26.31 %, respectively, higher than the control (Fig. 1B). Thus, acetic acid treatments up to 2 % might work as a buffer solution which enhances larval activity to consume proteinaceous substrates, leading to the increase of larval growth. In addition, it was reported that adding acetic acid can enhance fiber decomposition and nutrient availability during larval conversion (as discussed later). On the
other hand, previous studies showed that BSFL is capable of surviving and developing on diets within a wide range of pH values (pH = 3–10) [35,36]. It was noticed that in batch-rearing mode, the growth of BSFL improved and was heavier under slightly acidic conditions (i.e. pH = 5). Similarly, Kim et al. [44] reported that the performance of Drosophila larvae was improved by adding acetic acid to its diet. They suggested that acetic acid has a positive function in promoting gut microbial diversity and larval development. The gut microbiota of an organism plays a crucial role in various aspects of its physiology, including protein digestion, nutrient absorption, and immune system regulation [45]. An increase in microbial diversity is often associated with a healthier gut environment, which can enhance the overall insect performance [46]. In addition, several studies revealed that organic acids including acetic acid can regulate the gut physicochemical condition, feeding behavior, immune deficiency innate immune pathway, lipid metabolism, insulin signaling, and larval development in model insects such as Drosophila and honeybees [47,48]. Adding acetic acid was reported to alter the culture structure and biofunctions of gut microflora and facilitates the nutrient availability for larval growth. However, further increase in acetic acid dose to 4 % in the present study resulted in suppression of BSFL growth. This could be due to the biotoxicity of the culture medium at high acetic acid levels, partially inhibiting the BSFL conversion. This finding is consistent with the study of Pang et al. [22] who reported that
doses more than 20 g L-1 of acetic acid and 6 g L-1 of butyric acid in BSFL diet result in biotoxicity of the feeding medium due to inhibition of digestion and BSFL growth.
BSFL performance and waste reduction
Table 1 shows the performance criteria of BSFL grown on the mixed MBM and wheat bran supplemented with different doses of acetic acid. Overall, these findings support the hypothesis that acetic acid addition can improve substrate diet decomposition and larval conversion efficiency. An earlier study using fresh chicken manure [28] showed a minimum 14-day conversion time, compared to a shorter time in the current study. This implies that the substrate consisting of mixed MBM and wheat bran is more suitable for larval feeding. pH is one of the most important variables that affect the BSFL biotransformation by influencing the gut microorganisms and process performance. In addition, the initial pH effects can promote beneficial bacteria, improving the survival rate, larval development, and larval biomass acquired [21,43].
