An increase in pH often occurs during BSFL biotransformation, which is due to the biological conversion of N-containing organic compounds into ammonia, leading to a rapid increase in the pH of larval residues. At the end of bioconversion, the final pH value in the control was 8.93, which may be due to ammonia produced during conversion which increased the pH in larval residues (Table 1). However, the lowest significant pH values were recorded at 2 % to 4 % acetic acid doses, which were 23.1 % and 31.7 %, respectively, lower than the control. It can be noted that there is no significant difference in the pH value of these treatments, confirming that larval growth could compensate the reduction in pH caused by adding acetic acid. Thus, adding acetic acid to the substrate neutralizes and reduces the pH increase, thereby facilitating N reservation. BSFL showed notable substrate reduction potential, which was enhanced by acetic acid dietary supplements, whereas DMR significantly increased at acetic acid dosages of 1 % and 2 % (40.55 % and 42.38 %, respectively) in comparison to the control (37.42 %, Table 1). It is similar to previous reports, which indicated 35.9 %–43.7 % reduction in the substrate during the conversion of organic waste by BSFL [28]. The performance efficiency of BSFL for the digestion and conversion of the tested feed can be evaluated through the values of DCE, BCR, and BCR [49]. Results revealed a maximum DCE of 28.35 % at 2 % acetic acid addition, which was 12.10 % higher than the control. In addition, biomass conversion rate (BCR), representing the quantity of dry larval biomass acquired with a given quantity of diet, can be used as another indicator [49]. It can be confirmed that the two most significant BCR values (11.23 % and 12.02 %), representing 18.70 % and 27.10 % over the control, were obtained at 1 % and 2 % acetic acid dosages, respectively. Another indicator that reveals the effectiveness of BSFL in converting the applied diet’s protein is the protein conversion rate (PCR). Adding 1 % and 2 % acetic acid significantly enhanced the PCR to 34.74 % and 36.21 %, respectively. In this regard, when acetic acid was added at 1 % and 2 % doses to the rearing medium, the pH changes were relatively small compared to the control medium (Table 1). Thus, enhancement of larval biomass performance parameters in the presence of 1 % and 2 % acetic acid might be attributed to a positive effect as a buffer capacity of rearing medium [16,37], which makes the substrate more readily to be converted by BSFL. However, higher ratios of acetic
acid (3 % and 4 %) resulted in stress to the larvae, which hindered the assimilation of nutrients, leading to lower larval performance parameters, which agrees with a previous study [22]. Thus, by employing MBM as the main feedstock, the present results suggested 2 % acetic acid as the optimum dosage to improve BSFL biomass gain and growth performance.
Protein and lipid accumulation
Protein and fat are the two most important components that can be accumulated in the BSFL biomass during development. Many studies have shown that BSFL contains a wider range of protein and fat, reaching 30 %–46 % protein as stated previously [18,15] and 20 %–43 % lipids [19,50]. These levels vary depending on the diet’s composition, rearing conditions, and development phases that significantly influences the amount of lipids or protein accumulation, and the final weight of BSFL biomass. By providing a balanced diet rich in organic acids, glycerol, protein, and carbohydrates, these features can be tailored to optimize the bioconversion process [33]. In this regard, the effect of acetic acid doses on the nutritional components of BSFL is presented in Fig. 1C. Increased acetic acid doses caused significant changes in the amount of total protein (P < 0.05), which was higher than the amount of raw MBM (41.62 %), and reached its highest significant value of 44.87 dw% at 2 % acetic acid, which was 7.93 % higher than the control. Protein content at 2 % acetic acid dose is consistent with the results of Lopes et al. [51], who found that 45 dw% protein was obtained from BSFL reared on mixed aquaculture waste with bread waste. However, further increase of acetic acid dose to 4 % resulted in reduction of crude protein content to 40.83 %. Conversely, by increasing acetic acid doses, the crude lipid content increased gradually, reaching the highest significant value of 32.56 dw% at 4 % acetic acid dose (Fig. 1C). Comparatively, Pang et al. [22] recorded 19.45 dw% lipid content from BSFL reared on pig manure with corncob at the optimal concentration of SCFAs solution (acetic acid 20 g/L, with butyric acid 6 g/L), which is much lower than that recorded in the present study in the control (26.61 %) or all treatments. It can be attributed to the different types of diet and different concentrations of applied acetic acid. Accumulation of lipids due to addition of acetic acid may be r