Changes in larval frass characteristics
Raw MBM and BSFL frass samples from the control and after supplementation with 2 % acetic acid were microscopically investigated and analyzed by FTIR (Fig. 2). The raw MBM appeared as well-ordered particles with a compact structure and regular shape (Fig. 2A). However, BSFL frass appeared as disorganized granules with a rough surface and uneven shape (Fig. 2B) due to partial degradation of macromolecules during bioconversion process. Interestingly, BSFL frass samples treated with 2 % acetic acid dose revealed rough, split fragments with more noticeable wrinkles on the surface (Fig. 2C), which supports the loss of structure induced by acetic acid. As a result of employing acetic acid, the granules become smaller, increasing the granules surface area, improving the efficiency of degradation, and increasing the BSFL growth rate. One of the most typical applications for evaluating modifications in organic matter is infrared spectroscopic analysis, which studies the structural alterations in functional groups. Peaks and functional groups were found in the current study using Sigma-Aldrich database [52].
Aliphatic primary amines are associated with the peak near 3454 cm 1, which is brought on by N-H stretching. This peak in frass samples had the most intensity indicating a partial increase in protein removal during BSFL treatment (Fig. 2). The amine group’s N-H bending in proteins or molecules that resemble proteins is similarly related to the absorption
band at 1630 cm 1, which was marginally enlarged after BSFL conversion. Likewise, it can be noticed that acetic acid addition resulted in some new or modified peaks in the frass sample, such as peaks at 1380
and 870 cm 1, which are associated with C––O and C–H bending [28]. The raised strength of these peaks in frass samples is related to the composition of acetic acid, which is primarily made of C–H. The presence of these peaks in the frass sample indicates that carboxymethyl bonds in polysaccharides were partially eliminated [28]. Overall, the current findings confirmed that acetic acid supplementation improved BSFL performance, resulting in many modifications in the BSFL frass after conversion. Relative to FTIR spectra, the EEM spectra may provide additional details about the molecular decomposition of organic compounds from BSFL frass on various biomass [53]. Fig. 3 shows the EEM spectra of DOM obtained from raw MBM and BSFL frass samples from the control and after supplementation with 2 % acetic acid, which was further divided into five regions [54]. Based on DOM concentration measurements, the fluorescence absorption intensities of raw MBM in the five spots were significantly higher than those of BSFL frass samples. For 2 % acetic acid frass, absorption peaks largely appeared in areas III and V, indicating fulvic acid- and humic acid-like compounds as DOM’s primary constituents in these regions, respectively. In addition to regions III and V in raw frass, substantial absorption peaks also appeared in regions of II (protein-derived compounds) and IV (microbial byproducts). This suggests that addition of acetic acid accelerated the breakdown of proteins during BSFL rearing and facilitated the stability and humification of BSFL frass. In addition, the fluorescence response percentage (P i,n) results, the region III + V P i,n values in 2 % acetic acid
frass (83 %) were significantly higher than those in raw MBM (48 %) as shown in Table S2 (Supplementary data), which shows a greater humification level by acetic acid addition. The aforementioned findings indicated that 2 % acetic acid could increase the stability and humification of BSFL frass while reducing the fraction of aromatic protein-like substrates.
Fatty acid profile and biodiesel production
There are many kinds of saturated and unsaturated fatty acids majorly accumulated by oleaginous BSFL. It is confirmed that fatty acid profile in BSFL biomass mostly changes by the content and composition of the diet’s [33]. In the present study, fatty acid compositional profiles in BSFL lipids were varied with supplementation of acetic acid. Dominant fatty acids in the control were C12:0, C16:0, and C18:1, representing the most of the total fatty acids (Table 2). However, BSFL-reared acetic acid showed a different fatty acid profile where C12:0 increased to 47.20 % with 4 % acetic acid. In total, saturated fatty acids (SFAs) in the control, 1 %, 2 %, 3 %, and 4 % acetic acid doses represented 61.36 %, 64.10 %, 65.83 %, 65.75 %, and 75.15 %, respectively. In addition, monounsaturated fatty acids (MUFAs) proportions showed 35.37 %, 34.36 %, 33.45 %, 33.94 % and 24.11 % in the control, 1 %, 2 %, 3 %, and 4 % acetic acid doses, respectively, with insignificant changes among treatments, excluding the highest applied dose (4 % acetic acid). The ratios of polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) were the lowest values, ranging from 3.27 % to 0.31 % within the control and 3 % acetic acid dose, respectively (Table 2). These results indicate that acetic acid induces a stress response in the BSFL that alters their lipid metabolism
