[56] and biodiesel obtained using soybean oil (3.80 mm2 s 1) [26]. Moreover, the estimated cetane numbers (60.10–61.55) in the current study is higher than that of biodiesel created from BSFL grown using chicken manure (53.0–56.61) [28,56] and rapeseed cake (59.48) [33]. The higher cetane number is a desired feature because it refers to fast ignition of biodiesel with lower undesired emissions [57]. This gain also delivers a variety of further advantages to the internal compression engines, such as improved fuel efficiency, higher power output, and faster burning, all of which boost engine performance. The long-chain saturated fatty acids (LCSF) and the degree of unsaturation (DU), which are affected by the number of double bonds in various fatty esters, have a significant impact on the biodiesel stability and longevity [26]. Reduced DU in the lipid feedstock was reported to produce biodiesel with improved longevity [58]. Therefore, biodiesel obtained from the BSFL reared on MBM supplemented with acetic acid could enhance the stability of the produced biodiesel due to lower DU (Table 3). In this study, BSFL biodiesel showed moderate values of LCSF (2.18–2.96) and DU (0.26–0.42) compared to other BSFL biodiesel or soybean biodiesel. Other estimated BSFL-derived biodiesel characteristics, such as higher heating value, specific gravity, and iodine value, all fall within a narrow range of the international biodiesel standard EN 14214 and US ASTM, which are similar to those of biodiesel originating from soybean oil [26]. These attributes position biodiesel derived from BSFL as a promising and potent approach to mitigate the impact of land use associated with biodiesel production from terrestrial oil crops. Considering the discussed properties of BSFL-based biodiesel and its prior conversion benchmarks, 2 % acetic acid dosage is identified as optimal for maximizing BSFL biomass productivity while also meeting desirable biodiesel specifications.
Emission reduction
Fig. 4 shows the boxplots of daily changes in CO2, NH3, N2O, and CH4 emissions flux due to BSFL bioconversion by rearing on MBM and wheat bran enriched with different acetic acid doses. The major sources of ammonia and GHGs emissions generated during BSFL bioconversion are impacted by several key factors, such as diet quality, larval density, and microbial activities that contribute significantly to the share of CO2 emissions [59]. The results of this study showed that CO2 emissions are in the interquartile range of 10.0–18.7 g CO2/kg dw.day, with a median of 12.5 g CO2/kg dw.day in the control. The increasing variation in CO2 emission flux observed with different acetic acid doses during BSFL bioconversion process can be attributed to nutrient availability and the development of bacterial community. As a result, microbial respiration mainly contributes to CO2 emission and the variances in the survival of larvae among acetic acid doses. However, since larvae grow almost exponentially and thus their respiration would rise accordingly, it is also anticipate near constancy in CO2 fluxes [60]. The highest cumulative CO2 flux in the present study was recorded under 1 % acetic acid
(136.07 g kg 1 dw), while minimum cumulative CO2 flux was observed
at 4 % acetic acid (70.17 g kg 1 dw). Analysis of variance (ANOVA) and multiple comparison tests indicated a significant reduction (P < 0.05) in CO2 fluxes with further increase in acetic acid dose to 3 % and 4 % (Fig. 4a). This is probably due to the high acidity of the medium containing these doses which is unfavorable for microbial growth and BSFL activities. This is in consistence with previous studies evaluated emissions from BSFL bioconversion of agricultural manure and food waste [15,16]. Results illustrated in Fig. 4b show the NH3 daily emissions during BSFL bioconversion. It has been reported that NH3 is produced largely as
a result of BSFL excretion of uric acid during metabolism [20], which can be converted to NH3 gas by microbial activity. Additional factors that may affect NH3 production may include the humidity, temperature, aeration, and pH value of the medium [61]. It was clear that NH3 emissions pattern from the control increased gradually and peaked on
the 4th day by 0.53 g NH3/kg dw.day, with an interquartile range of 0.12–0.44 g NH3/kg dw.day, then it decreased slowly until the last experimental day. However, increasing of acetic acid dose significantly decreased the daily NH3 emissions, reaching 0.05 g NH3/kg dw.day on
the 8th day recorded at 4.0 % acetic acid. The maximum cumulative NH3
emissions in the present study were recorded under control (2.68 g kg 1 dw), while cumulative NH3 emissions observed with 2 %, 3 %, and 4 % doses were significantly lower than the control by 39.18 %, 64.74 %, and 79.70 %, respectively (P < 0.01; P < 0.001; P < 0.0001, post hoc Tukey’s test). The highest rate of NH3 emissions from the control is most likely contributed to the high rates of uric acid excretion during BSFL metabolism, followed by microbial ureases degrading urea and leftover
proteins into ammonium (NH4+) [61], which drives the pH shifts to more alkaline conditions, making the ammonium-ammonia balance in the direction to produce more NH3 emissions (Fig. S2, Supplementary data). However, addition of acetic acid to the substrate can play two major roles in reducing ammonia. First, it reduces the activity of microbial
ureases in the residues to avoid extra NH4+ [20]. Second, it can react with
NH4+to produce ammonium salts, resulting in a significant reduction in NH3 gas release [60]. Thus, sequestration of ammonia in the form of ammonium acetate for re-uptake and assimilation by BSFL takes place. These results also confirmed the improvement of larval metabolic performance due to acetic acid enrichment, which resulted in significant improvements in BSFL biomass and their biochemical composition. According to the findings of this study, BSFL emits relatively very low levels of N2O and CH4. The box plots of daily changes in N2O and CH4 emission patterns are shown in Fig. 4c and d. The N2O and CH4 cumulative emissions were in trace quantities for all treatments. It is in line with the recent findings of Parodi et al. [61] and Chen et al. [62], who reported that N2O or CH4 emissions were near zero after 7 days of BSFL bioconversion of pig manure. However, the comparison test showed a significant reduction (P < 0.05) in both N2O and CH4 fluxes at acetic acid doses of 3 % and 4 %. Moreover, the maximum cumulative N2O and CH4 emissions flux in the present study were recorded in the control, representing 1.5 and 0.96 g/kg dw, respectively. In comparison to conventional composting, N2O or CH4 levels in the BSFL bioconversion were significantly reduced. CH4 missions are directly related to the existence of methanogens and the abundance of Archaea [63,64], which deoxidizes CO2/H2 with acetic acid under anaerobic conditions. The main source of N2O generation is from anoxic and denitrification process, which is traditionally performed by denitrifiers. Based on the suggested gases’ routes (Fig. S2), the low emissions of N2O or CH4 might be attributed to two ways. First, the crawling and motion of the larvae improve aerobic conditions during conversion through continuous mixing of the substrate [28], which inhibits the activity of denitrifiers and Archaea, avoiding the production of N2O and CH4. Second, the microflora in the gut of BSFL have not been correlated with the production of CH4 and N2O, which was reported by Lee et al. [65]. The current study focused on assessing the direct emissions from the BSFL bioconversion in reducing GHGs and NH3 emissions using various acetic acid dosages. Despite CO2 being a significant GHGs in BSFL bioconversion processes, CO2 emission from the bioconversion of biowastes is typically not considered in the assessment of the global warming potential (GWP) since this CO2 is produced from a biogenic origin [60]. However, quantifying CO2 and NH3 emissions are still important for a better understanding of the distribution of both C and N during BSFL bioconversion. Therefore, only CH4 and N2O were considered when estimating total GHGs emissions. Using CO2 equivalents as a GWP100 of CH4 and N2O was 34 and 298, respectively [66]. The summary of gas emissions, including CO2, NH3, CH4, N2O and GWP100 per kg of dry matter BSFL biomass from the current study and published
