Conclusions
The current study achieved the improvements of BSFL performance with mitigation of greenhouse gas and NH3 emissions due to incorporating dietary acetic acid into the MBM, resulting in considerable changes in their growth and biomass components. The results showed that MBM with wheat bran mixed with 2 % dietary acetic acid could effectively mitigate GHGs and NH3 emissions through BSFL rearing. In addition, dietary acetic acid enhanced the availability of nutrients from MBM, which considerably promoted the larval productivity from 128.5
g m 2 d–1 in the untreated group to 162.3 g m 2 d–1 using 2 % acetic acid dose. A positive correlation between acetic acid addition and BSFL lipid accumulation was also confirmed. Moreover, 2 % acetic acid enhanced the mitigation of GHGs gases, which resulted in a GWP of 2.67 g CO2-eq per kg dry larval biomass. Additional research is required to comprehensively explore the mechanisms behind the reduction in emissions facilitated by BSFL. In conclusion, the current study introduces Hermetia
illucens as a mini-livestock with a vista potential for efficient recycling of waste protein from animal industry byproducts for improved biodiesel production and reduced GHGs emissions via a net waste-free approach that promotes a low-carbon economy and the sustainability targets.
